Dear Mr. Markulin,

Re: Viscoelastic (VE) Coupling Dampers (VCDs) for 4430-4488 Halifax St. 
 Our File: K23-028

We are pleased to present this Fee Proposal / Letter of Agreement for the detailed design and optimization of the Viscoelastic Coupling Damper (VCD) for the above-referenced building on behalf of Kinetica. We look forward to working with the entire design team and Kor Structural (Kor) to achieve an economical and reliable high-performance damping solution for this prominent project in Burnaby.


SCOPE OF THE PROJECT 

The scope of the project is to produce a detailed final design of the VCD including the viscoelastic (VE) damper panels optimized for cost and constructability through close interaction with Kor Structural (Kor) and damper manufacturers. 

At the end of this scope of work the following will have been accomplished:

EOR will have all necessary design information to be able to finalize the structural design of the entire building and submit drawings for permit. 
VE damper panel design will be finalized. 
VE damper panel drawings and VCD drawings will be finalized.

BOSA Developments will have all relevant information and documents to obtain a final binding quotation for fabrication and delivery of the VE damper panels including all final commercial and delivery terms.


At the end of the project, Kinetica will continue to support all parties to successfully implement the VCD in the project. 

SCOPE OF SERVICES 

The design will be separated into three phases: i) Preliminary design assessment with EOR, ii) Detailed VCD design and modelling information transfer and iii) VCD and damped building performance assessment. Following the design phases there will be quality control and site review phase which Kinetica will participate in as well. Each step is described below: 

Preliminary Design Development with EOR

When Kor has finalized the most up to date design models, Kinetica will perform the following work:

Kinetica and EOR will work closely to define the VCD configuration that achieves the damping requirements defined by the wind tunnel consultants. 

Kinetica and EOR will investigate the VCD configuration to achieve the overall performance goals on the project.

Kinetica and EOR will develop a steel connection detail that can provide the required strength and stiffness to connect the VCDs to the structure.

Kinetica will assist EOR in developing the structural performance requirements for the building with VCDs. 


VCD Design and Information Transfer

After approval from EOR on the design concept, Kinetica will provide the following services:

Kinetica will produce a final VE damper for the tower optimized for cost and performance and check all structural limit states according to AISC-360-16 and CAN/CSA S16-19 for the dampers. 

Kinetica will produce final VE damper panel drawings for manufacturing.

Kinetica will establish VE material properties for analytical models and assist EOR in doing their independent assessment of the level of added damping in the building within ETABS software.


VCD and Damped Building Performance Assessment

After the design has been finalized, Kinetica will carry out a state-of-the-art detailed performance assessment of the VCD and the damped building:

Kinetica will assess the damper performance within the overall structural model.

If requested by design team, Kinetica will conduct time-history analyses on the building within ETABS with EOR using wind time-histories provided by the wind consultant and further validate the damper performance using Kinetica’s state-of-the-art models and extensive test database.


Quality Control and Site Review

Following the design contract, Kinetica will continue to support the successful implementation on the project. The following will be included in this phase:

Kinetica will support EOR in developing the VCD Quality Control and Quality Assurance program including requirements for production testing and specifications. 

Kinetica will oversee the QA and QC program of the VE damper panel manufacturer. This includes visiting during fabrication and manufacturing of the dampers and reviewing and witnessing the full-scale testing. Kinetica will also review steel mill certs reports, batch viscoelastic material testing reports and full-scale testing during production of the VE damper panels. 

Kinetica will stamp and seal the VE damper panel drawings and design the bolted connection. Kinetica will also fill the B.C. P.Eng. Schedule S-B and S-C forms.

Kinetica will support EOR in developing the design of the connecting steel elements, lock-up connection and embedded portion of the connection of the damper panels to the structure.

Kinetica will review shop drawings of the VCDs and VE damper panels.

Kinetica will attend three site visits; typically these include a site visit to the steel fabrication facility of VCD connecting elements or site visits during the installation of VCD connecting elements and installation of the VE damper panels at the building site.


Required Information

Kinetica requires the following information to complete its scope of work.

From EOR:

Service Level Wind, Ultimate Level Wind and seismic ETABS models with the latest architectural and structural modifications. If any tangible structural changes are made an update of the structural models.

From Wind Tunnel:

Assessment of level of damping required to meet acceleration criteria based on the latest structural model by EOR. If ULS load reduction from damping is desired it is suggested to obtain wind tunnel time-history loads with the development of a full-storm duration and scaling factors to establish the final design VE material properties. 
KINETICA PROJECT TEAM 
The project engineering will be led by Dr.’s Montgomery, Guo and Madurapperuma and Mr. Joshua Tapia. Dr. Montgomery will be responsible for the detailed engineering on the 4430-4488 Halifax St. A brief bio of Drs. Montgomery, Guo, Zhong and Mr. Joshua Tapia are provided below. 
Michael Montgomery, PhD, PEng, Chief Technical Officer – Michael has been working with the VCD for 18 years and was Principal in charge of the Y+C Condos in Toronto, Park Central Towers (North and South), Connor Tower at Greenhills, Sunshine Fort Towers 1, 2, 3 and 4 and Parklink Towers 1 and 2, BWDC, 791 W Georgia, 1075 Nelson, 240 Adelaide and 33 Yorkville. He has 20 years of experience working on the design of a number of high-profile tall buildings worldwide. 
Jack Guo, PhD, PEng, Senior Associate and Seismic Risk Lead – Jack is an expert in seismic retrofit and seismic resilience and seismic assessment of buildings. He has worked on the seismic risk assessment of the Canadian Parliament Campus, Seismic Risk Study of 10 Tall Building Case Studies in the City of Vancouver and Canadian Centre for Inland Water.
Myron Zhong, PhD, EIT, Associate – Myron is an expert in the dynamic assessment of structures, modeling structures, controlled rocking systems, material modeling and assessments, laboratory upgrades, physical testing, shake table testing and control systems.
Josh Tapia, MASc, Associate – Josh is an expert in structural steel and testing of full-scale VCD steel connections at the University of Toronto. He is an expert in steel fabrication and design and will work on the steel element design for the project.

SCHEDULE 

Kinetica’s schedule for this work is anticipated to be the following:

Preliminary Design Development with EOR - 4 weeks

VCD Design and Information Transfer - 2 weeks

VCD Performance Assessment - 2 week

Quality Control and Site Review – 2 trips to site or fabricator for inspection



FORM OF AGREEMENT

Our Agreement will consist of this Letter, plus all Attachments and other documents referenced herein but not attached, including:"
