7f7413f3c8d9,01633-01,01633-01,Kerkhoff Construction Ltd.,Springfield & Ambrosi Kelowna L1 Slab,KOR,proposal,,,2023-02-21,projects,Projects\Projects\01 Small Jobs\01633-01 (1994 Springfield 1946 Ambrosi Rd Kelowna)\Documents from Kerkhoff\OneDrive_1_2-21-2023\641344 - PULLMAN Proposal 1994 Springfield_Rev01.pdf,,1,333 Sherbourne: Floor Strengthening,permits_approvals,303,"1 333 Sherbourne: Floor Strengthening 
 
• 
Location: Toronto, Ontario 
• 
Start Date: April 2022 
• 
Completion Date: July 2022 
• 
Contract Value: ~$1,890,000 (CAD) 
 
Project Description: The six (6) level structure located at 333 
Sherbourne in Toronto was undergoing a large internal 
renovation of level 05 and level 06 to install new patient rooms. 
As part of the building permit, the project team was required to 
strengthen the existing floor slabs to handle the increased live 
loads required by current building code(s). 
 
PULLMAN, in concert with STRUCTURAL TECHNOLOGIES, 
developed a comprehensive strengthening solution that 
involved strengthening the level 05 slab from the topside only. 
The level 04 area below was a fully occupied medical facility, 
including administrative office and clinical settings. There was 
no access permitted to strengthen the level 05 slab from below 
as relocating the level 04 occupants was too disruptive. 
 
PULLMAN developed a solution utilizing carbon fibre 
reinforced polymer (CFRP) fabric to strengthen the level 06 
slab from above and below. This included the application of 4-
hour ULC rated cementitious fireproofing material. The level 05 
slab was strengthened with a reinforced bonded overlay. This included the creation of a surface profile to 
CSP-5 and drilling/epoxying of over 12,000 rebar dowels to properly transfer in-plane shear forces. 
 
The most challenging portion of this project involved undertaking construction in a fully functional medical 
facility. PULLMAN was responsible for dust protection, transfer of materials & equipment, noise 
considerations and means-and-methods evaluation to limit disruption to occupants. The project was 
completed on-schedule with no safety related incidents and allowed Chart / UH to proceed with the 
construction of the much-needed medical facility. 
 
