• 
Location: Toronto, Ontario 
• 
Start Date: September 2021 
• 
Completion Date: December 2021 
• 
Contract Value: ~$390,000 (CAD) 
 
Project Description: Dufferin Transfer Station (DTS) is one of 
the largest waste transfer stations within the City of Toronto 
and is instrumental to the function of the City’s waste collection 
system. DTS receives, segregates, and transfers ten thousand 
tons of residential, commercial, and light industrial waste daily. 
In late 2020, the City of Toronto retained WSP to design a 
rehabilitation program to upgrade the facility for increased 
capacity handling. The City’s main requirement was to keep 
the facility fully functional during these repairs as diverting 
waste from DTS was not possible. 
 
PULLMAN, in concert with STRUCTURAL TECHNOLOGIES, 
developed a comprehensive design-build strengthening 
solution utilizing carbon fibre reinforced polymer (CFRP) 
laminate plates to strengthen the tipping floor slab from below. 
This included the application of 4-hour ULC rated cementitious 
fireproofing material to protect the CFRP. 
 
PULLMAN completed all-in place QC requirements, including 
subsurface adhesion testing, pull-off testing for bonded CFRP 
laminates, etc. 
 
Undertaking a repair program while the facility remained fully functional required tremendous preplanning 
amongst all project stakeholders. PULLMAN was able to develop an access scaffolding system that 
permitted adjacent access to building occupants, in addition to dust protection and noise considerations. 
The project was completed on-schedule with no safety or quality related incidents and allowed the city to 
continue with the operation of the facility. 
 
 
