PROPOSAL FOR PROFESSIONAL SERVICES FOR SEISMIC STRUCTURAL DESIGN PEER 
REVIEW COMMITTEE (SD-PRC) FOR A HIGH-RISE CONCRETE CORE WALL BUILDING 
 
Dear Mr. Dunham: 
 
I am pleased to respond to your request for a proposal for the Seismic Structural Design Peer 
Review Committee (SD-PRC) services for the project at 800 Broadway, San Diego. 
This proposal is based on my understanding of and experiences with the seismic peer review 
process used by the City of San Diego and other jurisdictions, as well as a brief review of 
preliminary information provided to me by sent by an email on April 1, 2020. 
In accordance with the City of San Diego requirements for building design based on alternative 
procedures (often referred to as projects utilizing a performance-based design approach along 
with nonlinear analysis), a Seismic Structural Design Peer Review Committee (SD-PRC) is 
required."
abeaa079958e,30940-01,30940-01,JWDA Inc.,800 Broadway San Diego CA,KOR,proposal,,,2020-05-07,projects,"Projects\Projects\03 Residential\30940-01 (800 Broadway, San Diego, CA)\01 General\Proposals & Contracts\Peer Review Panel\San_Diego_800_Broadway_BMZ_WallaceProposal_May_07_2020.pdf",,2,concrete flat-slab floor system that spans between the core and rectangular columns at the tower,permits_approvals,216,"2 concrete flat-slab floor system that spans between the core and rectangular columns at the tower 
perimeter. The building height is approximately 411.5 ft above grade to the roof level. The typical 
tower footprint provides anr area of approximately 11,000 SF. 
Because the tower exceeds 240 feet in height, the lateral system of the tower will be designed 
using a non-prescriptive seismic design approach as justified by Section 104.11 of the California 
Building Code (CBC) and Section 129.0109 of the Municipal Code. This code provision permits 
the application of alternative lateral forces procedures using rational analysis based on well-
established mechanics in lieu of prescriptive code provisions. The alternative procedure for the 
City of San Diego is typically based on a two-level design, including a service level earthquake 
(SLE) performance check and a maximum considered earthquake (MCE) performance 
assessment. The governing codes for the design will be the 2019 CBC and ASCE 7-16, along 
with ACI 318-19. A building-specific “Basis of Design’ Document will be developed to guide the 
design and review process; this document includes information on the approaches and 
procedures that will be used and well as criteria that will be used as a basis for accepting the 
analysis and design results. 
 
 
Fig. 1: (a) Project Section Elevation Rendering, and (b) Tower Plan View"
