structural responses for design for strength and occupant comfort. 
WIND TUNNEL MODELING
Wind tunnel testing is the most accurate means of predicting project-specific wind loads and responses, including wind-
induced motions. A scale replica of the project and the immediate surroundings will be tested in a wind tunnel which 
simulates wind speeds and turbulence levels appropriate for the site. 
Scale Model Construction & Wind Tunnel Testing
A scale model of the project site will be constructed, and one of two 
well-established rigid model techniques will be used to predict the 
peak responses. While both techniques are generally equivalent, 
RWDI will select the approach based on the needs of the project: 
High Freqency Force Balance (HFFB): The building is mounted on 
a balance capable of measuring base bending moments, shears 
and torsion from which modal forces can be calculated. This 
technique tends to be faster, but it is also better suited to 
situations with details such as balconies, screens or trellises. It is 
also well suited to shaping workshops. 
High Frequency Pressure Integration (HFPI): Taps, which measure wind pressure, will be installed on the surface of 
the model. Instantaneous pressures over the building surface will be integrated to form the base bending moments, 
shears, torsion along with the modal forces. This approach is best suited to simple façades but it also allows more 
flexibility to deal with peak loading on portions of complex structures as well as inclusion of higher order modes. 

Analysis & Deliverables
The test data will be analysed with the building’s structural 
properties (i.e., dynamic mass, stiffness and damping) and 
the wind climate model to determine the peak responses of 
the structure as a function of return period. Wind force 
and torque distributions will be provided along with 
appropriate combinations of sway and torsion for the 
design return period(s) of interest. The accelerations at the 
top occupied floor will also be predicted and compared to 
motion criteria for human comfort. Parametric runs will 
also be included to gauge the sensitivity of the responses to 
changes in key structural parameters."
