Page 2 of 6 
 
Our team has extensive experience in designing both 5-storey and 6-storey wood-frame buildings, so we 
are experts in the special design issues related to these structures. The Building Code prohibits shear 
wall transfers in wood-frame buildings over 4-storeys, hence we recommend consulting with us early 
during initial schematic design, so that important shear wall elements can be integrated into the unit plans 
and overall building massing. 
 
We have designed numerous similar projects, including market and rental housing, staff housing, social 
housing, health care, complex care, assisted living, and various other mixed-use projects. Over the past 
25 years we have completed projects at SFU and UBC and look forward to the opportunity of being 
involved with a project at Capilano University. 
 
Our proposal assumes that engineering design and drafting work for the 2 buildings will be done 
concurrently; however, construction of the 2 buildings may be completed in 1 to 2 phases with phased 
portions of our Construction Administration fee. We can provide a further breakdown of our proposed fees 
per building should it be required. 
 
Preliminary work and preliminary consultations with the Architect may be charged on an hourly basis in 
accordance with the included ‘Appendix A’ Schedule of Standard Hourly Billing Rates, however, please 
note that all charges for preliminary work are included in the proposed Professional Services Fees. Our 
fixed fee does include for attending site meetings; however, we are on site regularly once construction 
begins (inspecting rebar), so attending these meetings only when we are required is appreciated. 
 
Over and above the noted proposed fixed fees, we recommend a budget allowance of $1,500 for normal 
disbursements (e.g., courier costs), not including printing and plotting. 
 
Project Personnel & Experience 
 
The project engineer and principal contact for this project will be Kevin Wurmlinger, P.Eng. Struct.Eng., 
CEng, MIStructE. Kevin is a specialist Structural Engineer licensed in British Columbia with more than 20 
years of experience designing a wide variety of buildings and types, including tall towers, mixed-use 
commercial and residential projects, and office buildings, with expertise in concrete, steel, and timber. 
Kevin is involved in all stages of his projects from conceptual design through construction. He has 
extensive experience in structural design solutions, consultant team coordination, drawing preparation, 
site reviews, as well as construction administration, which has continuously led to the successful delivery 
of his projects. 
 
After the initial stages of design are complete, Kevin Wurmlinger will be assisted by senior partner, John 
Markulin, M.Eng., P.Eng. Struct.Eng., PE, SE, who is a licensed Structural Engineer in British Columbia, 
Alberta, California, Washington, Arizona, and Texas, and has more than 30 years of experience 
designing a wide variety of buildings, including tall towers, mixed-use commercial and residential projects, 
light industrial warehouses, and institutional/public facilities such as ice rinks, sports facilities, extended 
care facilities, hotels, and office buildings. John Markulin is responsible for delivering innovative structural 
engineering solutions for some of the firm’s most notable projects. John is an accomplished engineer with 
significant project experience in the design of reinforced concrete, pre-cast concrete, cast-in-place 
concrete, post-tensioned concrete, tilt-up concrete, structural steel, load-bearing steel stud, wood-frame, 
and masonry, as well as floor systems, vertical load systems and seismic and lateral systems. 
 
Kevin Wurmlinger and John Markulin will collaborate during preliminary stages of the work, which will 
include preliminary design concepts and preliminary design drawings for budgeting. Both Kevin and John 
will be available and remain fully involved in the project until construction is complete. 
 
 
 

