If either party is delayed or prevented from the performance of any of its obligations 
hereunder as a result of any act of god, strike, lockout, or labour disturbance, war, riot, 
insurrection, civil commotion, fire, flood, or other event or circumstance which is not 
reasonably foreseeable and which has not been caused by an act, omission, or 
negligence of, and is beyond the reasonable control of, the party relying upon this 
provision (each a ""Force Majeure Event""), that party is not liable for its failure in 
performance to the extent and for so long as performance is so delayed or prevented, 
provided that party gives notice to the other party promptly (and in any event not later 
than 48 hours) after becoming aware of the event or the circumstance delaying or 
preventing performance. A delay or failure by a subcontractor or a supplier of the 
Vendor or any of their respective subcontractors or suppliers of any tier, shall not 
constitute an event of Force Majeure hereunder. If as a consequence of a Force Majeure 
Event, a party has failed to perform its obligations under this Purchase Order for a 
continuous period of at least 30 days, the other party may, upon written notice, 
terminate this Purchase Order and the Purchaser shall pay for all Goods delivered and 
Services performed for which Acceptance has occurred to the date of such termination 
at the prices and on the terms and conditions set forth in this Purchase Order, but is not 
otherwise liable to the Vendor."
